  "update": {
    "key": "doc_315d02ff658e5615bd31fa3c6f37b793",
    "title": "Assigned portfolio icons automatically from tags",
    "summary": "Made Portfolio Editor assign a shared icon when it created an update, prioritizing the subject of the work over technology and work-type labels.",
    "date": "2026-02-22",
    "prominence": "medium",
    "external_url": "https://github.com/Ckrest/PortfolioSite",
    "preview": {
      "src": "media/screenshot_2026-02-28_00-00-07.png",
      "description": "Portfolio updates with automatically assigned icons",
      "width": 1372,
      "height": 903
    },
    "tags": [
      "Python",
      "Flask",
      "Automation"
    ],
    "relationships": {
      "part_of": "doc_45e24fee0f645130a7d9ce1685a5af01",
      "related": [
        "doc_1a3826668acb53688961faa991a157f1"
      ]
    },
    "blocks": [
      {
        "body": "## Give every imported update an icon\n\nPortfolio Editor had created compact updates without an icon, leaving their timeline cards with broken or generic presentation. I added a deterministic selector to the creation path. It checked domain tags first, skill tags second, and copied the selected asset from the shared icon library. When nothing matched, it used the shared code icon as a valid fallback.\n\nWork-type labels such as “Feature” and “Bug Fix” were ignored because they described the activity rather than its subject. A one-time backfill assigned icons to 24 existing updates that lacked them.",
        "type": "text"
      },
      {
        "description": "Domain, skill, and work-type tags passed through a fixed priority order before Portfolio Editor copied the selected shared icon into the update.",
        "presentation": "intrinsic",
        "src": "media/tag-icon-selection-flow.svg",
        "type": "image"
      },
      {
        "body": "## Make the choice repeatable\n\nThe selector lowercased incoming tags, classified them with the shared tag registry, and walked fixed priority tables. The mapping order—not the order of the incoming tags—decided the winner. An update tagged with both Wayland and Python received the desktop-layers icon because a domain match took priority over a skill match.\n\nBefore copying an icon, the selector verified that the selected asset existed in the shared library.",
        "type": "text"
      },
      {
        "code": "1. Matched a domain tag such as Wayland, ComfyUI, or Audio\n2. Otherwise matched a technology or skill tag\n3. Ignored generic work-type tags\n4. Fell back to the shared code icon",
        "description": "The priority order favored what the work was about over how the report classified the activity.",
        "filename": "selection-priority.txt",
        "language": "text",
        "presentation": "content",
        "sourceMode": "inline",
        "type": "code"
      },
      {
        "body": "## Preserve editorial control\n\nAutomatic selection ran only when an update did not already have an icon and no custom icon path had been provided. Existing choices stayed in place.\n\nBecause the selected SVG was copied into the update during creation, later changes to the mapping did not silently rewrite reviewed items. The automatic result remained an initial proposal that an editor could replace.",
        "type": "text"
      },
      {
        "description": "The maintained icon vocabulary and the resulting update cards show the deterministic mapping in use.",
